async #getHighestCount(): Promise<number> {
    const result = await this.#emailModel.findPage({
        filter: `created_at:<=${new Date().toISOString().split('T')[0]}`,
        order: 'csd_email_count DESC',
        limit: 1
    });

// For high volume senders (400k+), cap the increase at 20% or 75k absolute
if (lastCount >= WARMUP_SCALING_TABLE.highVolume.threshold) {
    const scaledIncrease = Math.ceil(lastCount * WARMUP_SCALING_TABLE.highVolume.maxScale);
    const absoluteIncrease = lastCount + WARMUP_SCALING_TABLE.highVolume.maxAbsoluteIncrease;
    return Math.min(scaledIncrease, absoluteIncrease);
}

for (const threshold of WARMUP_SCALING_TABLE.thresholds.sort((a, b) => a.limit - b.limit)) {
    if (lastCount < threshold.limit) {
        return Math.ceil(lastCount * threshold.scale);
    }
}

findings.body:
The new high-volume branch starts at `>= 400_000`, but the 2× tier below it still goes up to 400k. That means the limit drops as the previous peak crosses the threshold. A peak of 399,999 gives `ceil(399999 × 2) = 799,998`. A peak of 400,000 gives `min(480,000, 475,000) = 475,000`. A sender whose best send was one message larger gets about 325k fewer messages allowed on the next send. That is the opposite of the PR's goal of avoiding aggressive jumps and of keeping large senders from falling back into warm-up.

::diff{file="ghost/core/core/server/services/email-service/DomainWarmingService.ts" lines="119-129"}

Either the high-volume cap should apply from a lower point, so that the 2× tier never produces a value above the capped one, or the 400k tier should become the same capped rule. Pick one and make the boundary continuous.

The progression test asserts `{lastCount: 400000, expected: 800000}`, with the comment `400000 × 2 = 800000`. But the service routes 400,000 into the high-volume branch, which returns `min(ceil(400000 × 1.2), 400000 + 75000) = 475,000`. The test fails at this row and stops the rest of the progression from being checked. The expected value also matches the old 2× rule rather than the new behaviour the PR describes.

::diff{file="ghost/core/test/unit/server/services/email-service/domain-warming-service.test.ts" lines="203-212"}

The expectation should follow whatever boundary fix is chosen in the service. If the 400k row stays in the high-volume branch, the expected value is 475,000 and the comment should be updated to say so.

The query filter changed from `created_at:<${today}` to `created_at:<=${today}`. The unit test still checks `callArgs.filter.includes(`created_at:<${today}`)`. The new string does not contain that substring, because the character after `<` is `=`, so the assertion fails.

::diff{file="ghost/core/core/server/services/email-service/DomainWarmingService.ts" lines="100-104"}

The `<=` change is also unexplained in the PR. With a bare date literal, `created_at <= 'YYYY-MM-DD'` compares against midnight, so it only differs from `<` for rows stamped exactly at 00:00:00. The doc comment on `#getHighestCount` says it excludes today. Either revert to `<` or keep `<=` and say why, then update the test to match.

::diff{file="ghost/core/test/unit/server/services/email-service/domain-warming-service.test.ts" lines="181-181"}

`#getTargetLimit` calls `WARMUP_SCALING_TABLE.thresholds.sort(...)` on every call. That mutates the shared module-level table, and the array is already written in ascending order, so the sort does nothing useful. The fallback `return` after the loop is also documented as unreachable, which is only true because the `highVolume` branch runs first. A future edit that reorders the table will break that quietly.

::code{file="ghost/core/core/server/services/email-service/DomainWarmingService.ts" lines="131-138"}

Since the table is static, sort it once at definition or simply rely on the order. Consider deriving the 400k boundary from `highVolume.threshold` instead of repeating the number in two places.
